"""
HTML (HyperText Markup Language) read back as Markdown: CommonMark with the
tables, strikethrough and task lists of GFM (GitHub Flavored Markdown), in
the form the editor writes.  The input is the HTML that Wolly.Markdown
writes or that the editor CKEditor 5 stores.

The tokens come from parseHtml of Standard.Html; a tree of the elements is
built from them with the end tags that HTML implies (a new paragraph, list
item, row or cell closes the open one), then written out without recursion.

Mapping: p is a paragraph, h1 to h6 an ATX heading (after atx, the format of
Aaron Swartz: number signs before the text), strong and b strong emphasis, em and i emphasis, del, s and strike
strikethrough, code a code span, pre a fenced code block whose info string
is the language-x class of pre or of its code, a with href a link, img an
image, ul and ol (with start) lists, li an item, blockquote a quote, hr a
thematic break, br a hard break, table a pipe table of GFM with the first
row as its header and the alignment of the classes text-left, text-center
and text-right or of the style text-align.  A list item whose first content
is a checkbox input (checked for done), or whose class names task (and done),
is a task item.  u, sub, sup, mark, kbd and a span without attributes stay
as raw inline HTML.  div, figure, section, article and the like are block
boundaries, label and unknown elements are transparent, figcaption is a
paragraph of its own.  script, style, template, iframe, object, embed, svg,
math, form and the form controls go with their content; comments and the
doctype are dropped.

Text: runs of white space outside pre are one space, cut at block edges;
the backslash, asterisk, underscore, backtick, brackets, angle brackets,
tilde, pipe and ampersand get a backslash, and so do a number sign, plus,
minus and equals sign at the start of a line and the dot or parenthesis
after digits there.  A URL (Uniform Resource Locator) of a link or an image
is kept only when isSafeLink of Wolly.Markdown accepts it; otherwise the
link is its text and the image its alt text.

Output form: blocks are separated by one blank line (the items of a list
whose items hold no paragraph by none), bullets are minus signs, numbers
count up from the start, every line ends with a line feed and no space
except inside a fenced code block, and the text ends with one line feed;
empty input gives the empty text.

Limits: the first mostHtmlBytes bytes are read.  Lists and quotes nested
deeper than mostHtmlDepth are written into the deepest level, and so are
inline marks.  Elements nested deeper than 256 are read as if their tags
were not there, their content kept.  A dropped element that is never
closed takes the rest of the input with it.  Not mapped: definition lists
(their parts are paragraphs), colspan and rowspan, a table in a table
cell (its cells run into the cell), blocks in a cell (one line), inline
styles, the adoption of misnested inline tags that HTML does, and a link
or image whose URL isSafeLink refuses.

    let markdown: String := htmlToMarkdown("<h1>Title</h1><p><strong>Hello</strong></p>");
"""
import Standard.String (
    String,
    length
);

module Wolly.HtmlToMarkdown is
    """
    The most bytes of HTML that htmlToMarkdown reads: 1048576.  Longer
    input is cut there.
    """
    function mostHtmlBytes(): Index
        ensures result = 1048576;

    """
    The deepest nesting of lists and quotes written: 16.  What lies deeper
    is written into the deepest level.
    """
    function mostHtmlDepth(): Index
        ensures result = 16;

    """
    The Markdown of html, after the rules of the module head.  Any bytes
    give a text; empty input gives the empty text.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function htmlToMarkdown(html: Span[Nat8, R]): String
        ensures (spanLength(html) > 0) or (length(&result) = 0);
end module.
